So we are going with `removeChildren` instead of `removeAllChildren`? Anyways; there is a bug which I noted in the code. 

The problem with `removeChild` as it currently stands is that it forces the user to implement safety checks. So the user's code ends up getting pretty nasty:

``` js
if ( container.children.indexOf( other ) !== -1 ) {
    //will incur a redundant indexOf lookup
    container.children.removeChild( other );
}
```

Or, even uglier, to avoid the redundant indexOf:

``` js
var i = container.children.indexOf( other );
if ( i !== -1 ) {
    container.children.removeChildAt( i );
}
```

To sum up:
- `removeChild` should not throw any errors. Instead, it should return an indicator of whether the removal was a success. This can be true vs. false, or the removed child vs. null. 
- `removeChildAt` should still throw an error if the index is out of bounds, since it indicates programmer error

They don't have to do the checks, they are done for them internally. If the child is not of the parent they pass it to, it will throw an error. I would expect `removeChildAt` and `removeChild` to act the same, it doesn't make sense for one to throw an error and one to return a falsey value. They should both throw or both return, but not mixed in.

I would consider trying to remove a child from a parent it isn't in as a fatal error, so throwing is OK to me. Also to check if something is a child of a container, you don't need to use indexOf:

``` javascript
if(child.parent === container) {
    //true, but unecessary check since we will do it for you and throw if false
}
```

@englercj 
The thing is, the user has to do the checks one way or another to avoid their whole program crashing due to an uncaught exception. So you either need a try/catch or an ugly and non-obvious `if` statement. 

I guess a lot of it comes down to perspective. IMHO the goal of `removeChild` is to ensure the container no longer contains the specified child. In this regard; it's not a fatal error if the child has already been removed, or if it's been removed and added to another container. 

Another reasoning would be convention; the majority of JavaScript exceptions are there to signify programmer error (like index out of range and null pointer checks), rather than "I ran into something that you may or may not care about." Using try/catch disables optimization in V8 (and possibly SpiderMonkey?), so JS developers tend to be pretty weary of using them. 

In the _exceptional_ case where the user cares whether an element was successfully removed, it can still be handled cleanly with my suggestion:

``` js
var success = container.removeChild(child);
if (!success) {
    // do something exceptional... 
}
```

@mattdesl I'm not sure I agree with that logic, if you are trying to remove a child from a container and it fails because that child is in another container your scene just got potentially _very_ dirty. Having an exception will catch logic errors when trying to improperly remove objects.

I think it is an exceptional case to not care if an element removed successfully. To say "remove this from the scene, but if it doesn't actually remove I don't care" seems wrong to me. I also don't think `if(child.parent) child.parent.removeChild(child);` is all that dirty for the case where you want to check first. I actually do that a couple times in grapefruit (in case the user moved things around they shouldn't have). It has actually been pretty helpful to see an error when I tried to remove something incorrectly, since it meant I was doing something wrong when it happened.

That being said, this one of the few places we throw real errors and I generally like to avoid actual exceptions in browser code whenever possible. I'd be fine with returning `null` when the child is not within that container, and the child that was removed when successful. I'd be more than happy to discuss more on a PR that changed these to not be errors, though since it was @GoodBoyDigital's decision to use errors I would want him to at least be aware of the change before I actually merged it.

@SukantPal, I think the work that you've done here is terrific. I commend you for optimizing these code paths related to caching bounds, because it's subtle, difficult work.

One of the challenges of PRs like this, is that I (and probably others on the PixiJS team), don't fully understand the extent to which this may produce unwelcome side-effects for developers. It's one thing add unit-tests, it's another to do a full integration where users heavily rely on bounds.

Our goal as an open source library is, most importantly, is to make sure we do no harm. I think it's important that you can work with developers (@themoonrat, @GoodBoyDigital, and others) to test this build into real-world, complex apps and games. As much as you're able to prove and show that this will not negatively impact users, it will be easier to approve.

